<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleanor Roosevelt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Anna Eleanor Roosevelt, First Lady of the World</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleanor Roosevelt was an American political figure, diplomat and activist, the longest-serving first lady of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, during her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>'s four terms from 1933 to 1945. A niece of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>, she married Franklin in 1905. As first lady she held regular press conferences and wrote a daily newspaper column, and argued for the civil rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and Asian Americans. Widowed in 1945, she was a United States delegate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> until 1952, chaired the UN Commission on Human Rights and oversaw the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a>, adopted in 1948. She died in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-roosevelt.jpg" alt="Eleanor Roosevelt" width="330" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleanor_Roosevelt_portrait_1933_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Lincoln Memorial: she had been barred from singing at the DAR Constitution Hall under its whites-only policy, and after a national outcry, and with the support of <b>Eleanor Roosevelt</b>, the concert was held on the memorial's steps before more than 75,000 people and a radio audience of millions.</blockquote><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a></li><li><blockquote>The drafting committee was chaired by <b>Eleanor Roosevelt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
